cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or the results it produces, please cite the associated paper."
title: "Placement-Specific Axis Reduction of Wearable Inertial Sensors for Gym Exercise Recognition"
abstract: >-
  Evaluation of 15 axis configurations, from the full six-axis inertial set
  through every three-axis subset, sensor pair and single axis, on the public
  RecGym dataset at three body positions (wrist, pocket, calf), scored by
  macro-F1 under leave-one-subject-out validation. Axis-level reduction is
  shown to be a placement-specific decision rather than a universal saving.
type: software
